    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-69393-1",
    "abstract": "Global warming and socio-economic development are together prompting a surge in the use of air-conditioning (AC). Yet the technology that delivers thermal comfort also emits large quantities of greenhouse gases (GHG), exacerbating climate change. We quantify global AC-related GHGs and associated warming impact under five climate scenarios, separating the contributions of global warming and socio-economic development. In a middle-of-the-road scenario (SSP245), cumulative AC-related emissions reach 113.3 GtCO<sub>2</sub>eq between 2010 and 2050, increasing global-mean temperature by 0.05 \u00b0C (0.03 \u00b0C-0.07 \u00b0C), with only about 8.3% to climate-driven cooling demand. Income inequalities exacerbate disparities in AC use, substantially limiting access to cooling in lower-income regions. While rising incomes reduce this inequality, they increase emissions: income-driven AC growth adds 14-146 GtCO<sub>2</sub>eq and a further 0.003-0.05 \u00b0C of warming by 2050, even under SSP119. These results highlight the need for a rapid low-carbon cooling transition that balances total warming impacts with equitable cooling access.",
    "field": "Climate impact of air conditioning",
    "problem": "Existing studies have not quantified the feedback loop between rising air-conditioning use and global warming, distinguished between socio-economic and climate drivers, or systematically assessed how these interactions could shape future cooling inequalities under diverse climate scenarios.",
    "method": "We develop an integrated assessment framework that links improved cooling demand projections to the GCAM and MAGICC models to quantify the total warming impacts and resulting inequalities of global air-conditioning use across future climate scenarios.",
    "task": "The task is to quantify the global warming impact from rising air-conditioning use under various socio-economic scenarios and to assess how income inequalities affect greenhouse gas emissions and access to cooling.",
